 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot k^2)$ per image per iteration, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $k$ is the kernel width [S1]. The use of separable kernels reduces this to $O(N \cdot k)$ [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(k^2)$ for storing the kernel, or $O(k)$ for separable kernels [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves 19.72 PSNR on DTU 3-view, improving over the Baseline (19.54) [S3].
-*   **Scaling:** The method is effective for reducing training iterations (e.g., 50k vs 200k) when combined with other efficient spectral filtering algorithms [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes camera alignment, restricts high-frequency noise, and improves robustness in joint optimization [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Handcrafted schedule may not fully align frequency relationships compared to adaptive methods [S3].
-*   **When to Use:** When joint optimization of camera poses and scene representation is unstable or prone to high-frequency artifacts [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When adaptive frequency alignment is required for optimal results [S3].
+- **Time Complexity:** The addition of 2D Gaussian blur increases the per-iteration cost. However, using separable convolution reduces this to $O(K \cdot W \cdot H)$ per image, where $K$ is the kernel size [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Minimal additional memory, as the smoothed image can be computed on-the-fly or cached [S1].
+- **Measured Performance:** On DTU 3-view, Smoothed 2D Supervision (Adaptive Gaussian Blur) achieves 19.72 PSNR, improving over the Baseline (19.54) [S3].
+- **Scaling:** The method is effective for joint optimization of camera poses and scene parameters, reducing training iterations required for convergence (e.g., 50k vs 200k in some MLP-based methods) [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Stabilizes camera alignment, restricts high-frequency noise, and improves robustness in early training stages [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Handcrafted schedule may not fully align frequency relationships between positional encodings and pixel supervision compared to adaptive methods [S3].
 
 ## Background
-Smoothed 2D Supervision was introduced as part of techniques to improve the robustness of joint optimization in radiance fields and Gaussian Splatting [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is inspired by the analysis of spectral modulation in frequency regularization [S2, unofficial]. The method was experimentally proven crucial for successful pose refinement in ablation studies [S1]. It is used in methods such as Trick-GS [S4] and Adaptive Rendering Loss Regularization [S3].
+Smoothed 2D Supervision was introduced as a technique to enhance the robustness of joint optimization in differentiable rendering [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is inspired by the analysis of spectral modulation in radiance fields, where blurring the 2D training images helps stabilize the optimization process [S2, unofficial]. The method has been applied in various contexts, including joint pose refinement [S1] and efficient Gaussian Splatting training [S4].
 
